WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.740
Welcome back to mCoding!  
Hope&nbsp;you're having a great day.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.716
attrs is a library to help you 
write Python&nbsp;classes

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:12.487
in a simpler, less error-prone, 
and less&nbsp;boilerplate way.

00:00:12.487 --> 00:00:16.310
It offers similar functionality&nbsp;
to the built-in dataclasses library.

00:00:16.310 --> 00:00:21.120
And in fact, dataclasses 
were originally based on&nbsp;attrs.

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:23.213
Very commonly, 
when I write a class,

00:00:23.213 --> 00:00:29.056
I need to define an __init__, __eq__, 
a __repr__,&nbsp;maybe a __hash__, __lt__ etc.,

00:00:29.056 --> 00:00:34.143
And most often, all those functions 
are complete boilerplate.

00:00:34.143 --> 00:00:35.501
Who&nbsp;can even remember all of them?

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:41.251
And if I do, yeah, maybe 
procrastinate on actually filling them in.

00:00:41.251 --> 00:00:45.300
__init__ means self.something= something, 
for each argument,

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:50.330
__eq__ means check 
self.something= other.something, and&nbsp;so on.

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:52.519
If I want to add 
or remove an attribute,

00:00:52.519 --> 00:00:55.628
I now have to add or remove 
it in all of&nbsp;these places,

00:00:55.628 --> 00:00:59.386
which is both a huge waste of my time
 as well as a very likely place

00:00:59.386 --> 00:01:02.507
where I make a 
typo and break everything.

00:01:02.507 --> 00:01:06.214
Enter attrs and other 
dataclass like solutions.

00:01:06.214 --> 00:01:09.287
Instead of writing all that, 
they write it for you.

00:01:09.287 --> 00:01:14.580
Delete all that flaky, error-prone&nbsp;code. 
Here's how you write it with attrs.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.740
Let's watch that again because&nbsp;
it was so satisfying. Nice!

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:23.899
You can also customize the generated
 code using&nbsp;parameters to define

00:01:23.899 --> 00:01:28.440
or by using these&nbsp;field 
objects on individual attributes.

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.740
This is implemented by 
posting wrong&nbsp;code to Stack Overflow

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:34.000
and waiting for&nbsp;someone 
else to write it for you.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:36.840
Just&nbsp;kidding, 
it's this `@define` decorator.

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.773
It could also be done 
with a metaclass.

00:01:38.773 --> 00:01:43.345
But attrs and dataclasses both take 
the decorator approach instead.

00:01:43.345 --> 00:01:46.049
At this point, you may feel your 
knee jerking&nbsp;upwards,

00:01:46.049 --> 00:01:48.979
telling you that this is 
too much forbidden magic.

00:01:48.979 --> 00:01:50.127
But fear not.

00:01:50.127 --> 00:01:55.638
After the&nbsp;decorator runs, the result is the
 plain old class that you would have written yourself.

00:01:55.638 --> 00:02:00.807
We can even&nbsp;inspect, say, the __init__ method 
to see the source code it generated:

00:02:00.807 --> 00:02:03.470
self.something = something, and&nbsp;so on.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:06.180
Exactly the boilerplate we wanted to avoid.

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:08.795
No magic is happening when 
you use the class.

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:11.287
The&nbsp;plain old boring repetitive 
code is still there.

00:02:11.287 --> 00:02:15.230
And the resulting class is just as&nbsp;
efficient as if you had handwritten it.

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:18.120
It's just that you didn't have 
to handwrite it.

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.744
While the logic of 
this decorator is complex,

00:02:20.744 --> 00:02:23.533
ultimately all it's doing is 
using your annotations

00:02:23.533 --> 00:02:27.709
and whatever parameters to write&nbsp;
the source code of each function in the class.

00:02:27.709 --> 00:02:32.760
And then it executes that code to create the&nbsp;
function objects and stick them onto the class.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:38.000
But pretty much everything I've said&nbsp;applies 
just as well to dataclasses&nbsp;as it does to attrs.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.740
So why do I prefer attrs?

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:43.327
To be clear, dataclasses 
are still great.

00:02:43.327 --> 00:02:45.751
And&nbsp;once they came out when 
they were first released,

00:02:45.751 --> 00:02:51.004
I started using attrs less and less just due to their 
convenience&nbsp;of being built into Python.

00:02:51.004 --> 00:02:53.965
But in the past year, the tables 
have really started to turn.

00:02:53.965 --> 00:02:57.540
And I'm&nbsp;choosing attrs over data 
classes more and more.

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.533
The first reason is slots, 
which is kind of&nbsp;a superficial thing

00:03:01.533 --> 00:03:04.763
because both attrs and 
dataclasses support slots.

00:03:04.763 --> 00:03:10.393
But attrs make&nbsp;slotted classes the default, 
and good defaults are surprisingly important.

00:03:10.393 --> 00:03:13.214
I have a whole video&nbsp;on slots 
if you want to check out the details.

00:03:13.214 --> 00:03:18.869
But the short version is that normal Python&nbsp;classes are 
basically syntactic sugar around a dictionary.

00:03:18.869 --> 00:03:24.780
And just like a dictionary, they can&nbsp;
have arbitrary new keys/attributes added to them.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:28.828
In contrast, a slotted class has 
a fixed&nbsp;set of instance attributes,

00:03:28.828 --> 00:03:30.828
which makes it slightly 
more efficient,

00:03:30.828 --> 00:03:35.049
but also, this&nbsp;prevents a very 
common bug from making a typo.

00:03:35.049 --> 00:03:39.708
If I accidentally try to set 
`user.nmae= JAMES`

00:03:39.708 --> 00:03:41.907
instead of getting an error 
on a normal class,

00:03:41.907 --> 00:03:48.180
I would just silently create the `nmae` attribute&nbsp;
on my instance, leaving the old name unchanged.

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:52.546
There's no error. I just now 
have incorrect data&nbsp;in my application.

00:03:52.546 --> 00:03:57.729
Can't wait to ship it to a customer, only for 
them to tell me every name&nbsp;in a database is null.

00:03:57.729 --> 00:04:01.740
But on a slotted class, as soon as 
I try to set the `nmae` attribute,

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:04.142
I get an error telling me 
I've done goofed again,

00:04:04.142 --> 00:04:07.363
telling me `nmae` isn't 
a valid attribute.

00:04:07.363 --> 00:04:12.857
While&nbsp;it's certainly possible that you want the ability to 
dynamically create new attributes on your class&nbsp;at runtime,

00:04:12.857 --> 00:04:16.733
it's far more common for classes to
 know all their attributes ahead of time.

00:04:16.733 --> 00:04:19.620
So&nbsp;it makes sense for slots 
to be the default.

00:04:19.620 --> 00:04:25.200
But like I said, attrs and dataclasses&nbsp;
both support slots, so what's the problem?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.598
This brings me to my second reason: 
Versioning.

00:04:28.598 --> 00:04:32.109
Dataclasses are part of the 
Python standard library,

00:04:32.109 --> 00:04:37.020
As Python develops, more and more&nbsp;features 
like slots are added to dataclasses.

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:41.478
But if I want to use those features, 
I need&nbsp;to upgrade my version of Python,

00:04:41.478 --> 00:04:47.820
which may involve convincing a client to upgrade 
and/or&nbsp;drop support for an older version of Python.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:49.649
That might not be acceptable.

00:04:49.649 --> 00:04:53.378
And in a world where&nbsp;many companies 
are still using Python 3.8,

00:04:53.378 --> 00:04:59.220
that means no slots if I'm using dataclasses&nbsp;since 
they aren't supported until Python 3.10.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:02.829
In contrast, if attrs introduces&nbsp;
a new feature that I want to use,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.829 --> 00:05:08.422
all I need to do is bump the version of attrs 
on&nbsp;a per-project basis, which is super easy to do.

00:05:08.422 --> 00:05:16.297
And reasons three, four, five, and so on are all&nbsp;of the features 
that attrs supports that&nbsp;dataclasses just doesn't.

00:05:16.297 --> 00:05:24.603
In particular, attrs supports validators, converters, 
and factories that take a `self` parameter.

00:05:24.603 --> 00:05:28.888
And it allows&nbsp;you to set 
specific `on_setattr`  hooks per attribute.

00:05:28.888 --> 00:05:34.200
So you could, for example, make only&nbsp;
one field frozen but allow the others to change.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:38.438
Another superficial one: attrs recognizes&nbsp;
the importance of immutability

00:05:38.438 --> 00:05:44.400
and gives you a top-level `@frozen` alternative 
to define&nbsp;that just sets the frozen parameter to true.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:48.723
These are all little things. But when 
you use&nbsp;a tool like this every single day,

00:05:48.723 --> 00:05:50.562
these little things really 
start to add up,

00:05:50.562 --> 00:05:54.840
which is why I&nbsp;find myself using 
attrs more and more in 2023.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:57.510
Let me know what you think! 
Do you&nbsp;use attrs?

00:05:57.510 --> 00:06:02.223
What about dataclasses, or do 
you prefer handwriting all your classes?

00:06:02.223 --> 00:06:04.847
Thanks for watching! 
Thank you to my patrons and donors.

00:06:04.847 --> 00:06:09.900
Slap that like button an odd number&nbsp;of times.
And I'll see you in the next one.

